  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{-\\frac{9}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$  \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/999a34650676174083aa479669eecca0c837dc8e5f1397a81a8949d8f9dc9de1.png)",
